  "protocolVersion": 1,
  "kind": "how-rainbows-form",
  "apiVersion": 1,
  "releaseId": "how-rainbows-form-v1",
  "description": "How a rainbow forms, for a child of about eight. Outdoor model: a low Sun, water drops (a rain shower or a fountain) and an explorer called Mia. A primary rainbow is every direction 42 degrees from the point opposite the Sun; Mia sees it only where sunlit drops lie in those directions and inside her view. rainbowStatus says why or why not: no_sunlight, no_drops, sun_too_high, drops_elsewhere (no drops opposite the Sun as seen from her spot), looking_away, rainbow. The rainbow is drawn only in Mia’s window, never in the meadow. Drop model: one round drop with real refraction, one reflection inside, and refraction out, for red, green and violet. Sizes and distances are illustrative; the Sun is drawn close. Not modelled: second bows, brightness, drop size, light that reflects more than once. Exact answers and assistance are stored separately from what the child says. No completion threshold.",
